   "id": "amd",
   "headline": "AMD's data centre chips nearly double in a year",
   "facts": [
    "Q3 revenue $13.4B +45% y/y",
    "Data Center $8.5B +97% on EPYC and MI450",
    "Anthropic, Meta, Microsoft, OpenAI, OCI on Helios",
    "Q4 guide ~$17B +/-3%",
    "After hours +7.7% (StockTitan 6pm ET)"
   ],
   "sources": [
    "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/0000002488/000000248826000188/q32026991.htm",
    "https://www.stocktitan.net/articles/stock-market-today-live-november-3-2026",
    "https://cnbc.com/2026/11/03/stock-market-today-live-updates.html"
   ],
   "paragraph": "A data centre is a warehouse of computers, and AMD sells two kinds of brains for them: server CPUs, which run general tasks, and GPUs, which do the arithmetic AI needs. AMD's Data Center revenue was $8.5 billion in its third quarter, up 97% from a year earlier, driven by EPYC server CPUs and the launch of Instinct MI450 GPUs. Anthropic, Meta, Microsoft, OpenAI and Oracle Cloud Infrastructure are running workloads on the first Helios systems, which pair MI450 GPUs with EPYC CPUs. AMD expects about $17 billion next quarter. What is unknown is how much of that depends on a few big customers, since the release gives no split.",
   "edited": "none",
   "explainer": "sonnet"
  },
  {
   "id": "arista",
   "headline": "Arista's network gear sales jump 58.5%",
   "facts": [
    "Q3 revenue ~$3.66B +58.5% vs $3.3B guidance",
    "Fastest growth since IPO",
    "After hours +13% (StockTitan)",
    "Margins/guidance not confirmed"
   ],
   "sources": [
    "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/0001596532/000159653226000252/ex991q326-earningsrelease.htm",
    "https://www.stocktitan.net/articles/stock-market-today-live-november-3-2026"
   ],
   "paragraph": "Thousands of AI chips are only useful if they can swap data quickly, so something has to connect them. Arista makes the network switches that link servers inside data centres, a bit like the junctions in a road system. It reported third-quarter revenue of about $3.66 billion, up 58.5% from a year earlier and above its own guidance of $3.3 billion, which it called its fastest year-over-year growth since its IPO. Shares rose about 13% after hours, after AI-linked stocks had pulled back in recent weeks over worries about slowing data-centre spending. Not confirmed: margins, profit and next-quarter guidance, so whether this pace lasts is an open question.",
   "edited": "Changed 'on a day when AI-linked stocks had pulled back' to 'after AI-linked stocks had pulled back in recent weeks' to match the packet",
   "explainer": "sonnet"
  },
  {
   "id": "anthropic",
   "headline": "Anthropic's IPO filing warns of AI that speeds up its own research",
   "facts": [
    "S-1/A released 2 Nov per TechCrunch",
    "AI in R&D could pose considerable safety risk",
    "Internal estimates for future models may soon meet RSP threshold",
    "No current model crossed",
    "Considering RSP updates; skeptics say marketing"
   ],
   "sources": [
    "https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/02/anthropic-warns-that-its-very-close-to-rsi-capability-aka-self-building-ai/"
   ],
   "paragraph": "Imagine a carpenter who builds better tools, then uses those tools to build even better ones, faster each round. Recursive self-improvement is that loop with AI: a system good enough at building AI that progress speeds up sharply. Anthropic's revised IPO filing says it already uses AI in its own research, and that this could pose a considerable safety risk if it sharply accelerates progress. Its internal estimates for future models suggest it may soon meet its own threshold for dramatically accelerated progress. No current model has crossed it, but Anthropic admits it may only notice an acceleration after it has happened. The filing gives no timeline and names no models, and skeptics say such warnings also work as marketing.",
   "edited": "none",
   "explainer": "sonnet"
  }
