  "lens": {
   "mechanics": "Gold competes with cash and bonds as a store of wealth. When a Treasury pays more after inflation, holding a metal that pays nothing costs more, so money leaves gold; when real returns fall or money is printed, it comes back. The template: late in a debt cycle, tight money squeezes borrowers until policy makers ease and devalue.",
   "data": "Three forces. One, real yields: the 10-year real yield rose to 2.65%, a solid return after inflation. Two, tight money: openings of 7.52M and CPI of 3.7% keep hikes priced. Three, the debt load (Long view) argues for easing later. Today the first two dominate.",
   "unsustainable": "Interest takes 21.9% of federal revenue while the Fed is still tightening. Like a family whose card interest grows faster than its pay, that only ends with spending cuts, more income, or cheaper money. How today differs: inflation is too high for the easing step.",
   "would_change": "Midterm results through Wednesday morning HKT; ISM services Wed 23:00 HKT; October payrolls Fri 21:30 HKT.",
   "check": "Debt-cycle read checked (Sonnet): 9 of 9 checks passed."
